WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:07.900
In this walkthrough we'll value a
single deal and see the components
behind the number, in the OpenTRMS Workbench.

2
00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:17.667
Run Valuation prices one deal on demand. It is the
surface to reach for when a number on a position
looks wrong and you want to see how it was arrived at.

3
00:00:17.667 --> 00:00:26.333
It takes a deal identifier. Every deal in
the Workbench carries one, and you can
copy it from the blotter or from a deal panel.

4
00:00:26.333 --> 00:00:36.100
The as-of date matters as much as the deal.
Valuing the same trade on two dates gives two
answers, because the market data behind it moved.

5
00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:45.733
The curve set decides which market data is
used. Leaving it empty takes the default
set, which is what most valuations should use.

6
00:00:45.733 --> 00:00:57.233
Asking for the component breakdown is worth doing. Without it
you get one number; with it you get the parts that made it,
which is what you need when you are checking rather than reporting.

7
00:00:57.233 --> 00:01:10.267
Run valuation submits it. The breakdown opens as its own
panel beside the form, so the inputs can be adjusted and
the valuation run again without losing what is already on screen.

8
00:01:10.267 --> 00:01:18.100
That completes Valuing a Deal. Every surface
shown here can also be opened from the agent
panel on the right, by asking for it in plain English.
